Hi,

> the priors I would put on intercept is a Cauchy(0,10) and a t-student (0,0.03) with 1 degree of freedom for the coefficients of the covariates.
>
> I try doing:
>
> inla_model <- inla (y ~ f(1,model='linear',, ) + f(AGE,model='linear') + f(CLASS,model='linear')+ f(INCOME, model='linear) -1 ,  data=df, family='binomial' )
>
> but where do I have to explicit the priors (and their parameters in the f()  supposing to fix location=0 and scale=10 aand the same procedure for t-student)? :(

Priors are defined in the ‘hyper’ argument inside the f() function. Before doing anything else, please, make sure that you know how priors are set in INLA [1] and try to use any of the other priors implemented in R-INLA for testing. If you use the muparser to define the prior, then the prior parameters will be set in the muparser code.

Best,

Virgilio

[1] https://becarioprecario.bitbucket.io/inla-gitbook/ch-priors.html

